{"step_index":956,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-02T18:20:33Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nCRITICAL FIX: Fix the comma truncation bug in numeric extraction.\n\nROOT CAUSE:\nThe Pydantic schema is defining rates and totals as float/int, which causes commas (e.g., \"2,535.00\") to be split or truncated to \"535.00\".\n\nCHANGE TO STRING SCHEMA & POST-PARSE:\n1. Update the Pydantic extraction schema so all currency/numeric fields are strings:\n   class InvoiceItem(BaseModel):\n       description: str\n       quantity: str\n       unit_rate: str\n       total_amount: str\n\n   class ExtractedInvoice(BaseModel):\n       vendor_name: Optional[str]\n       gstin: Optional[str]\n       invoice_number: Optional[str]\n       date: Optional[str]\n       items: List[InvoiceItem]\n       subtotal: Optional[str]\n       cgst: Optional[str]\n       sgst: Optional[str]\n       igst: Optional[str]\n       declared_total: Optional[str]\n\n2. Add a robust parse_inr_number helper in Python:\n   import re\n\n   def parse_inr(val) -> float:\n       if val is None:\n           return 0.0\n       # Keep only digits and decimal point, stripping commas and currency symbols\n       cleaned = re.sub(r'[^\\d.]', '', str(val).strip())\n       try:\n           return float(cleaned)\n       except ValueError:\n           return 0.0\n\n3. Add this instruction directly to the Gemini extraction prompt:\n   \"NUMERIC INSTRUCTIONS:\n    - Never discard digits before commas! '2,535.00' must be extracted as '2,535.00'. '1,270.00' must be extracted as '1,270.00'. '4,490.00' must be extracted as '4,490.00'.\n    - If you are unsure about the digits before the comma, read the 'Amount in Words' printed on the bill (e.g. 'FOUR THOUSAND FOUR HUNDRED AND NINETY RUPEES ONLY') to confirm the thousands digit.\"\n\n4. After Gemini returns, map every numeric field through `parse_inr(val)` before sending to the frontend or calculation engine.\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-11-02T23:50:33+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":957,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-02T18:20:33Z","input_tokens":3010,"cache_read_tokens":105497,"output_tokens":860,"thinking":"The numeric extraction is failing due to comma truncation. The Pydantic schema currently defines numeric fields as floats/ints, causing commas in values like \"2,535.00\" to be truncated. Updating the Pydantic schema to treat all currency and numeric fields as strings is necessary. This will allow the full string values to be captured, which can then be parsed.\n\n\nNow implementing the revised data schema that casts all numeric fields as strings. A Python helper function is added to parse Indian Rupee (INR) formatted numbers, handling commas and currency symbols to convert the string values to floating-point numbers. Added specific instructions to the extraction prompt, ensuring that the model does not truncate digits before commas and uses the \"Amount in Words\" to validate large numbers. These parsed values will be processed before they are used in calculations or sent to the frontend.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":60,"Path":"/Users/pranit405/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/audit-it/server.py","StartLine":35}}]}
